1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,887
Reliable regression starts before the first click. In this episode, design an

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:11,625
experiment whose starting data, identity, and cleanup belong to one test attempt. A

3
00:00:11,638 --> 00:00:15,925
new browser context removes browser state, but it does not reset money held by a

4
00:00:15,938 --> 00:00:21,838
backend service. Likewise, a fresh account does not remove cookies from a reused page.

5
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:27,713
You need both boundaries. We will trace the fixture from creation to deletion and

6
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:32,988
examine why retries, parallel workers, and authentication reuse can otherwise create

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,238
misleading outcomes.

8
00:00:36,462 --> 00:00:40,950
Use the local fixture endpoint to create a synthetic user, source account, and

9
00:00:40,962 --> 00:00:46,100
beneficiary with the agreed opening balance. Include a run identifier and case

10
00:00:46,112 --> 00:00:52,187
identifier for diagnosis. The server adds unique identifiers, so two learners can use

11
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,424
the same case name without sharing mutable accounts. Inspect the creation status

12
00:00:57,437 --> 00:01:02,674
before reading the response. Retain the fixture identifier immediately, because

13
00:01:02,687 --> 00:01:08,174
cleanup needs it even when the next setup step fails. Never replace targeted fixture

14
00:01:08,187 --> 00:01:11,637
creation with a shared account that someone manually reset earlier.

15
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:18,331
The fixture client carries a capability used only by this local training environment.

16
00:01:19,194 --> 00:01:22,919
Configure its hook key from the laboratory environment and restrict its use to

17
00:01:22,931 --> 00:01:28,181
preparation and deletion requests. The business context authenticates using the

18
00:01:28,194 --> 00:01:32,931
synthetic credentials returned for its fixture. Do not install the hook header

19
00:01:32,944 --> 00:01:38,068
globally on the browser. This separation clarifies what each observation proves:

20
00:01:38,306 --> 00:01:42,744
ordinary business requests must work with the user's session, while setup endpoints

21
00:01:42,756 --> 00:01:46,544
deliberately create the data needed for a repeatable classroom experiment.

22
00:01:48,754 --> 00:01:54,542
Login through the HTTP client associated with the browser context. After a successful

23
00:01:54,554 --> 00:01:59,979
response, the session cookie is available to that context's page. Use the same

24
00:01:59,992 --> 00:02:04,804
loopback hostname for login and navigation; changing between localhost and its numeric

25
00:02:04,816 --> 00:02:10,429
address changes the cookie's host scope. When a page unexpectedly redirects to login,

26
00:02:10,642 --> 00:02:16,142
inspect that relationship before changing timeouts. Avoid saving synthetic session

27
00:02:16,154 --> 00:02:21,666
files unless a specific exercise requires them. Per-test login keeps identity

28
00:02:21,679 --> 00:02:24,954
ownership visible and is sufficient for this small local workflow.

29
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:32,343
Some suites save authenticated storage state to avoid repeating login. That can be

30
00:02:32,355 --> 00:02:36,642
appropriate when tests do not collide through the same user's server data, or when

31
00:02:36,655 --> 00:02:41,892
each worker receives its own account. Reusing a session file alone does not isolate

32
00:02:41,905 --> 00:02:47,618
account balances or transfer histories. Before adopting this optimization, list every

33
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:52,793
mutable resource associated with that identity and decide who owns it. For our three

34
00:02:52,805 --> 00:02:57,430
central cases, fresh synthetic users are easier to reason about than a fast shared

35
00:02:57,443 --> 00:02:59,755
login followed by complicated repairs.

36
00:03:01,982 --> 00:03:06,895
A retry is another experiment, not a continuation that may reuse an already debited

37
00:03:06,907 --> 00:03:11,944
account. Arrange fixture setup so each attempt gets its own source account,

38
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:18,657
beneficiary, session, and idempotency key. Within the idempotency case, reuse the key

39
00:03:18,670 --> 00:03:23,807
only for the two requests representing the same intended transfer. Across test

40
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:29,670
attempts, generate a new intention and new data. Otherwise, a retry might succeed by

41
00:03:29,682 --> 00:03:33,870
replaying a transfer from the failed attempt, creating the appearance of stability

42
00:03:33,882 --> 00:03:36,982
while exercising a different path than the original test.

43
00:03:39,208 --> 00:03:44,233
Add an authorization extension after the three central cases: create two fixtures and

44
00:03:44,246 --> 00:03:49,420
try accessing one fixture's account with the other's session. The local contract

45
00:03:49,433 --> 00:03:54,420
defines a forbidden response for cross-fixture business access. Inspect both the

46
00:03:54,433 --> 00:03:59,721
response and the unchanged target state. This extension checks a different risk from

47
00:03:59,733 --> 00:04:05,046
insufficient funds. Keep it separately named and independently prepared so its purpose

48
00:04:05,058 --> 00:04:10,846
is clear. AI may suggest combining them for brevity, but a combined case often makes

49
00:04:10,858 --> 00:04:13,633
it harder to identify which rule actually failed.

50
00:04:15,858 --> 00:04:21,321
When a test fails, capture the relevant browser, API, and correlation evidence before

51
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:26,520
deleting the fixture. Then close the browser context and remove only the owned data

52
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:31,508
through the local deletion endpoint. Make the deletion operation idempotent so a

53
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:36,758
second cleanup attempt is safe for that fixture. Record its response separately from

54
00:04:36,770 --> 00:04:42,321
the original failure. Deleting the entire laboratory state may appear convenient in a

55
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:46,708
single demonstration, but it invalidates other running experiments and prevents

56
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:50,633
meaningful parallel execution across learners or language versions.

57
00:04:52,844 --> 00:04:58,119
Walk through a setup failure. Suppose fixture creation succeeds but authentication

58
00:04:58,132 --> 00:05:04,982
fails. The test body may never run, yet the created account still exists. The setup

59
00:05:04,994 --> 00:05:09,356
owner must therefore register cleanup as soon as creation succeeds, rather than

60
00:05:09,369 --> 00:05:14,832
waiting until every preparation step completes. Use the fixture mechanisms appropriate

61
00:05:14,844 --> 00:05:20,382
to your language and inspect their behavior during exceptions. If cleanup also fails,

62
00:05:20,494 --> 00:05:25,519
preserve the original cause and the remaining fixture identifier. A useful report

63
00:05:25,532 --> 00:05:29,856
explains both problems without converting either into a silently skipped success.

64
00:05:32,086 --> 00:05:37,286
Now imagine two workers submitting transfers at the same time. Each owns a browser

65
00:05:37,299 --> 00:05:42,799
context and a separate fixture, so their account assertions remain deterministic. The

66
00:05:42,811 --> 00:05:47,661
shared local services can process both requests, but the observations remain filtered

67
00:05:47,674 --> 00:05:52,761
by each source account and transfer identifier. Increasing worker count changes

68
00:05:52,774 --> 00:05:58,486
scheduling, not the business oracle. If failures appear only under parallel execution,

69
00:05:58,661 --> 00:06:04,148
compare fixture identifiers and session ownership first. Then investigate service

70
00:06:04,161 --> 00:06:08,524
behavior with correlated evidence instead of immediately adding global locks around

71
00:06:08,536 --> 00:06:09,398
every test.

72
00:06:11,616 --> 00:06:16,454
Give AI the setup and cleanup code together with the contract. Ask it to trace

73
00:06:16,466 --> 00:06:20,904
resource ownership through success, assertion failure, login failure, and an

74
00:06:20,916 --> 00:06:26,016
interrupted request. Require it to identify which fixture might remain and which

75
00:06:26,029 --> 00:06:31,466
evidence might be lost in each path. This is more useful than asking for generic best

76
00:06:31,479 --> 00:06:37,604
practices. Review every suggestion against the actual runner's lifecycle. If a

77
00:06:37,616 --> 00:06:41,816
proposed helper does not exist in your project, either implement it deliberately with

78
00:06:41,829 --> 00:06:45,354
clear responsibilities or reject that unsupported shortcut.

79
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:53,170
Your checkpoint is a short ownership checklist attached to one executed case. It names

80
00:06:53,183 --> 00:06:59,358
the run, attempt, fixture, source account, browser context, and cleanup result. It

81
00:06:59,370 --> 00:07:03,358
also explains how authentication reached the page and where failure evidence was

82
00:07:03,370 --> 00:07:08,383
captured. Review the checklist with a partner and ask them to predict what happens if

83
00:07:08,395 --> 00:07:13,845
login fails or the test is retried. When those answers follow directly from the code,

84
00:07:14,058 --> 00:07:18,083
you have a solid foundation for the distributed observations in the next episode.
